<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swabia (Bavaria)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bavarian Swabia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> is an administrative region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, often called Bavarian Swabia, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>. It covers the Swabian lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iller">Iller</a> and the Lech, including Augsburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kempten">Kempten</a> and Memmingen, which the Kingdom of Bavaria acquired in 1803–1810 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a>, prince-bishoprics and other territories. Its people speak Swabian dialects of Alemannic German.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria.jpg" alt="Swabia (Bavaria)" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zebra848 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Schwaben_Bayern.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> is one of the seven administrative regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, in the south-west of the state, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> as its administrative capital. It consists of ten districts and 340 municipalities, four of them cities. It is divided for planning into three regions: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a>, Augsburg and Donau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iller">Iller</a>, and the last also includes two districts and a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>. With the district of Lindau it has access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region is the eastern part of the former Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, which was once ruled by the dukes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen dynasty</a>. After the execution of the Swabian duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1268, his uncle, the Bavarian duke Louis, inherited some of Conradin's possessions in Swabia. In 1803, through the German Mediatisation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> acquired the further East Swabian territories, which it merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a>.</p><p>After the founding of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> the state was reorganised in 1808 into fifteen districts, created in the fashion of French departments and named after their main rivers. Territorial changes later reduced them to eight, and the Swabian lands with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a> formed the Oberdonaukreis, the Upper Danube District. In 1837 King Ludwig I renamed all the districts after historical territories and tribes of the area, and the Oberdonaukreis became Swabia. During the Nazi period the area was separated from the rest of Bavaria as the Gau Swabia, and it was reincorporated after the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> detached the town of Lindau from Bavaria in 1945, and it was reunited with the district in 1955. In 1972 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg an der Donau</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main sights include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> and several other old cities, among them Donauwörth, Nördlingen, Memmingen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindelheim">Mindelheim</a>, Kaufbeuren and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kempten">Kempten</a>. Ottobeuren Abbey stands in the region, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> runs through the north. The Allgäu Alps lie in the south, with the Hochfrottspitze and Hochvogel, and the royal castles of Hohenschwangau and Neuschwanstein stand near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fussen">Füssen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swabia (Bavaria)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the Imperial Recess (Reichsdeputationshauptschluss) of 1803 the right-bank lands, including Heidelberg and Mannheim, went mainly to Baden, with smaller parts to Hesse-Darmstadt, the princes of Leiningen and others, while Elector Maximilian IV Joseph was compensated in Franconia and <b>Swabia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Old Bavaria and <b>Swabia</b> were lands of large peasant farms, Catholic piety and village life, while Franconia and the Palatinate had a stronger Protestant presence, more crafts and a tradition of commerce.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Lieutenant Field Marshal Anton Sztáray, commanding the far right wing of the Austrian army in <b>Swabia</b>, defeated the French at Wiesloch in the War of the Second Coalition.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-3">3 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Merovingian kings then placed the Alemanni under dukes, a region covering modern Baden-Württemberg, Alsace, eastern Switzerland and <b>Bavarian Swabia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-alamannia">Duchy of Alamannia</a></li><li><blockquote>Buchau grew on an island in the Federsee in upper <b>Swabia</b> beside the abbey of noble canonesses founded there in the 8th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-buchau">Free Imperial City of Buchau</a></li><li><blockquote>Giengen grew on the river Brenz in eastern <b>Swabia</b> as a Hohenstaufen royal town.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-giengen">Free Imperial City of Giengen</a></li></ul></section>
